wn walking down the sidewalk and  seeing not the end of the rainbow but  the beginning of the rainbow curving up  and away from him and rather usefully  the rainbow had steps cut into it you  know um so that he he way he goes on  this journey over the rainbow and he  meets all kinds of weird creatures which  I actually mercifully I forget and my  dad I wrote it all out if my dad took it  to the office it got his secretary to  type it up so it became my first  typescript you know and then he said  well you know if I leave it with you  you're gonna lose it so I'm gonna look  after it so he looked after it he lost  it I had you see what I mean about the  uselessness of fathers and so I don't  have it anymore but that was the first  story that's what I meant when I said I  thought it made a writer for the first  time I actually sat down and wrote a  story and and I think one of the things  about that story that is like the  classic quest narrative and therefore is  quite like this book is that the the the  process of the quest is not just you  know overcoming problems it also has to  do with personal growth that the Dorothy  who becomes the Dorothy who kills the  which is a very different Dorothy than  the Dorothy who is unable to protect her  little dog you know and and the what  happens Dorothy is she discovers that  all the grown-ups are useless you know  auntie em Uncle Henry are pathetic they  can't even protect her against the  horrible miss Gulch  you know the The Tin Man is rusty and  the Cowardly Lion is cowardly and the  Scarecrow is made  straw and The Wizard of Oz is a fake you  know pay no attention to that man behind  the curtain  you know he's there all all the  grown-ups are pathetic and so she has to  step into the grown-up position you know  she has to do what the grown-ups can't  do and that business of growing up as  part of the quest it's true if you go  right back to you go back to the holy  grail you know you don't find the holy  grail until you are worthy of finding  the holy grail you have to become the  person who is considered pure enough Sir  Galahad for the Grail to manifest itself  to you so in all these in all the  classic quest stories there's that idea  of becoming better you know through  through adversary through adversity  through the overcoming of difficulties  and the defeat of goblins or dragons and  so on becoming better and and I think  that's sort of what happens at this book  - I think Lucca grows up you know it's a  bits about a boy growing up and and  becoming more able to handle the world  you know by the end than he is at the  beginning thanks  you spoke of people being more  enthralled with Darth Vader what do you  make of Hitchens embrace of Bush and  Cheney you're asking me to talk about  Christopher's politics yes I didn't  agree with him but the thing about  Christopher is he is one of my dearest  friends and right now we're thinking  about something else regarding  Christopher you know and where he's  engaged in another battle and my  feelings are there I don't give a damn  what he thought about George Bush you  know thank you  George Bush is toast anyway are we done  all right I think we're done thank you  folks  thank you  mr. Rushdie will autograph books on the  other side of the elevators and Dave  Barry's party is upstairs on the fifth  floor Terrace and we'll see you back  here tomorrow morning for Carl Hiaasen  and Scott Turow
 

